{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang|military}} A [[kilometer]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|author={{w|Dan Simmons}}| title={{w|The Fall of Hyperion (novel)|The Fall of Hyperion}}|edition=1st|location=New York|publisher={{w|Doubleday (publisher)|Doubleday}}|isbn= 0-385-24950-0 |lccn= 89037438 |oclc=20093277|page=165|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fireupondeep00ving_0/page/165/|passage=Several '''klicks''' closer, the ruins of the City of Poets rose jaggedly against the skyline. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=w:Robert J. Sawyer|title=Hominids|text=an asteroid between one and three kilometers wide had slammed into the ground at fifteen '''klicks''' per second.}}
#*{{quote-video game|en|developer=Bungie|authorlink=Bungie|title=w:Halo 3|publisher=w:Microsoft Game Studios|date=25 September 2007|platform=w:Xbox 360|deriv=1.0|level=[https://www.halopedia.org/Sierra_117 Sierra 117]|passage=Chief, can you hear me?...(''static'') My bird's down. Half a '''klick''' -(''static'')- downriver from your position.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|usually|in the plural}} [[kilometre|Kilometres]] per [[hour]].

=====Usage notes=====
Though kilometers are not commonly used to measure distance in the USA, {{m|en||klick}} is commonly used by the US & UK military, which use the metric system almost exclusively in order to facilitate communication with allied forces. (In other English-speaking countries, civilians often say "k" or "k.m." instead.)

